Certainly! Below is a JavaScript program utilizing some advanced features such as Promises, async/await, ES6 classes, and destructuring.

```javascript
 
const fetchUserData = () => {
    return new Promise((resolve) => {
        setTimeout(() => {
            resolve({
                id: 1,
                name: "John Doe",
                email: "john.doe@example.com",
                friends: [
                    { name: "Jane", id: 2 },
                    { name: "Mike", id: 3 }
                ]
            });
        }, 1000);
    });
};

 
class User {
    constructor({ id, name, email, friends }) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
        this.friends = friends;
    }

    getProfile() {
        return `Name: ${this.name}, Email: ${this.email}`;
    }

    async fetchFriendNames() {
        const friendNames = this.friends.map(friend => friend.name);
        return await Promise.all(friendNames);
    }
}

(async () => {
     
    const userData = await fetchUserData();

     
    const { id, name, email, friends } = userData;
    const user = new User({ id, name, email, friends });

     
    console.log(user.getProfile());

     
    const friendNames = await user.fetchFriendNames();
    console.log(`Friends: ${friendNames.join(", ")}`);
})();
```

This script demonstrates using Promises, async/await for handling asynchronous operations, ES6 classes with destructuring, and template literals for string interpolation.